It's a really weird experience trying to, like like take a tour of something it isn't there. It's you have to have a very active imagination, which I do. But if you want to try it find like a highway and just stand under it and then come here and then try to remember what that feels like. Back in the real world, things are quite nice around Lower Manhattan. Where we're standing now, it's open, there's trees over there, there's big buildings right here, none of this was a part. It's a little bit of a plaza, this is a nice diner here, you've got shops, you know, we're looking over there, there's townhouses and tenements that probably are asking way too much for rents because of where we are, that would have nothing. None of this. None of this would be here.
试图参观一个不存在的东西是一种非常奇怪的体验。你必须有非常活跃的想象力，我确实有。但如果你想尝试一下，找一条高速公路，站在它下面，然后来到这里，试着回忆那种感觉。回到现实世界，曼哈顿下城周围的情况相当不错。我们现在站的地方，很开阔，那边有树，这里有大楼，这些都不是那个计划的一部分。这里有一点广场，这里有一家不错的餐馆，你有商店，你知道，我们往那边看，有联排别墅和廉租房，因为我们所处的位置，租金可能高得离谱，如果那个计划实施，这一切都不会存在。这一切都不会在这里。

26
00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:43.000
Which brings us back to Andrew's map of Robert Moses' unbuilt Manhattan. I think what's most striking about these documents is how unstriking they are with their Google standard grays and blues and yellows. Andrew created a map to show how terrible these things would have been. But in creating the map, he was actually able to see the project from the planners point of view. As I went through cutting across Manhattan, before I drew in the highway, I just looked at the swath that I had erased. It was an interesting feeling. I could put myself in Robert Moses' shoes where I'm not looking at a city while I'm doing that.
这让我们回到了安德鲁的罗伯特·摩西未建成曼哈顿地图。我认为这些文档最引人注目的是它们是多么的“不引人注目”，有着谷歌标准的灰色、蓝色和黄色。安德鲁制作了一张地图来展示这些东西会有多糟糕。但在制作地图的过程中，他实际上能够从规划者的角度看待这个项目。当我穿过曼哈顿切割时，在画入高速公路之前，我只是看着我擦除的那片区域。这是一种有趣的感受。我可以把自己放在罗伯特·摩西的位置上，我在做这件事时并没有看着一座城市。

27
00:13:43.000 --> 00:14:03.000
I'm looking at a map. And because I don't see the people and I don't see the homes, I don't see the buildings, it's very tempting to just be like, okay, you just put it right there. You know, not realizing there's a building outlined here. And I can, I know what that building means. That's a place that people live, that people go that I've been to. And now it's off the map. It doesn't exist.
我看着的是一张地图。因为我看不见人，看不见家，看不见建筑物，所以很容易就会想，好吧，你就把它放在那里。你知道，没有意识到这里勾勒出了一栋建筑。我知道那栋建筑意味着什么。那是一个人们居住的地方，人们去过的地方，我也去过的地方。现在它从地图上消失了。它不存在了。

28
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:32.000
It's funny to hear you describe your own map with like a degree of animosity. I wanted to put this for the everyday person to look at this and say wow, that would have been terrible. I would have loved to be able to make a Google Street view and like put yourself right there so you could in this map stand under the highway, see what it was like, just have this great dark mass above you. The story of how all this didn't come to be is a long one. see what it was like, just have this great dark mass above you. The story of how all of this didn't come to be is a long one. It often centers around an activist named Jane Jacobs, who led a campaign to keep one
听到你用一种敌意来描述你自己的地图，这很有趣。我想把它呈现给普通人看，让他们说“哇，那太糟糕了”。我本来很想制作一个谷歌街景，让你置身其中，这样你就可以在这张地图上站在高速公路下面，看看那是什么样子，感受一下头顶上那巨大的黑暗块。关于这一切是如何没有实现的，故事很长。它通常围绕着一位名叫简·雅各布斯（Jane Jacobs）的活动家，她领导了一场运动，阻止了摩西的一条高速公路摧毁她心爱的街区——格林威治村（Greenwich Village）。

29
00:14:32.000 --> 00:15:05.000
of Moses' highways from destroying her beloved neighborhood, Greenwich Village. And there were other factors like the massive cost of those highways that were proposed that kept them from happening as well. But the interesting part to me is how that threat of the Lower Manhattan Expressway crystallized the story of Jacob's neighborhood. It's almost as if the threat from Moses made Greenwich Village into the place it now seems destined to have become. And that brings us back to the San Francisco Bay area.
还有其他因素，比如提议的高速公路的巨额成本，也阻止了它们的实现。但对我来说，有趣的部分是曼哈顿下城高速公路的威胁是如何让雅各布斯的街区故事变得清晰的。就好像摩西的威胁让格林威治村变成了它现在注定要成为的地方。这又把我们带回了旧金山湾区。

30
00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:33.000
Here's John King and Alison Ariath again. The unbuilt can in fact start what does happen. So if you drive north on the Golden Gate Bridge immediately to your left as you start to reach the end of the bridge, you see a tremendously beautiful hills overlooking the ocean, and then the bay to the other side. And there's nothing built on those hills. It's all natural landscape and there's people biking and walking and it's rolling hills
再次请出约翰·金和艾莉森·阿里亚斯。未建成的项目实际上可以决定后来发生的事情。所以，如果你开车在金门大桥上向北行驶，当你开始到达桥的尽头时，在你的左手边，你会看到俯瞰海洋的极其美丽的山丘，另一边是海湾。那些山上什么也没建。全是自然景观，有人在骑自行车和散步，那是连绵起伏的山丘，

31
00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:53.000
that go all the way out to the headlands. And when you see it for the first time, it's kind of hard to believe that this much open, beautiful landscape exists right next to one of the most expensive cities in the world. And it is kind of remarkable because obviously it would be some of the most prime real estate in the area. In fact, that's in the 60s what someone attempted to do there.
一直延伸到岬角。当你第一次看到它时，很难相信在世界上最昂贵的城市之一旁边，竟然存在着如此多开阔、美丽的景观。这确实非常了不起，因为显然那将是该地区最优质的房地产之一。事实上，这就是60年代有人试图在那里做的事情。

32
00:15:53.000 --> 00:16:22.000
And in the early 60s, a developer thought, what a great place to build 20,000 homes or so for people who are going to work in San Francisco. It was called Marincello. This was not seen as a rapacious idea at the time. This was seen as how business is done. It was a massive mix of houses and towers. And it was approved with very little controversy by the Marin County Board as supervisors.
在60年代初，一位开发商心想，在这里为那些在旧金山工作的人建造大约2万套住宅，该有多好。它被称为“马林塞洛”（Marincello）。在当时，这并不被视为一个贪婪的想法。这被视为商业运作的方式。这是一个巨大的住宅和塔楼混合体。它在马林县监事会几乎没有争议地获得了批准。

33
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:59.000
But Marincello being approved was enough to alarm people and start people reacting to it. And it led to lawsuits, it led to opposition, it led to local cities starting to fight it. When the dust settled the project did not get built. Apparently the only thing that was built was the gate that was going to lead to the marketing center at the very start of the property. So the Golden Gate National Recreation Area was formed out of concern for all the various plans that were being proposed. Marinchello was blocked, the Sutro-Baz lands end in San Francisco had a proposed kind of can-cune type resort.
但马林塞洛的获批足以引起人们的警觉并开始反抗。它导致了诉讼，导致了反对，导致了当地城市开始抗争。当尘埃落定时，该项目没有建成。显然，唯一建成的是那个原本通往物业入口处营销中心的大门。因此，金门国家游乐区（Golden Gate National Recreation Area）的成立，正是出于对所提议的各种计划的担忧。马林塞洛被阻止了，旧金山的苏特罗浴场（Sutro Baths）和土地尽头（Lands End）曾有一个提议的坎昆式度假村。

34
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:48.000
And if you are at all familiar with lands end in San Francisco where the Sutro-Bathroom are, you know how silly that notion sounds. It is only cancun as interpreted by the North Pole. You know, so there are various projects like that that what was proposed never got built, but it set the events in motion that in fact determine the landscape we have. What projects like that did in the early 60s is that they were a signal to people who cared about the topography of the Bay Area and the proximity of open space to urbanized space to say, what are we going to do?
如果你对旧金山的土地尽头和苏特罗浴场有任何了解，你就知道这个想法听起来有多愚蠢。那只是北极版诠释的坎昆。所以，有各种各样的项目，提议的从未建成，但它们引发的事件实际上决定了我们今天拥有的景观。60年代初这类项目所做的是，它们向那些关心湾区地形以及开放空间与城市化空间邻近性的人发出了信号，说：我们该怎么办？

35
00:17:48.000 --> 00:18:16.000
We can't fight every one of these battles that comes along. Is there a holistic way to go about it? And you had some very smart people who came up with the idea of, well, what if there was a national park that could take in all this? And Marin Cello may be unbuilt, but to say that there's nothing there in the headlands is not seeing the grand thing. San Francisco actually did build for itself, and everyone else who visits.
我们不能打赢每一场随之而来的战斗。有没有一种整体的方法来处理它？有一些非常聪明的人提出了一个想法：如果有一个国家公园能把所有这些都囊括进去呢？马林塞洛可能没建成，但说岬角那里什么都没有，那是没看到宏伟之处。旧金山确实为自己以及所有来访的人建造了一些东西。

36
